---
/*
 * The ACCENT REACH dimension, explained and, above all, shown. English twin of `pages/acento.astro`;
 * keep the two in step.
 *
 * THE SPECIMEN IS AN APP, NOT A GRID OF PARTS. Every reach token appears where it actually lives:
 * the current item in a sidebar, the counter in a navbar, the tags and the link in a content panel.
 * A row of loose badges proves the colour changes; a whole interface proves what the dimension
 * claims, which is how much of a SCREEN ends up dressed in the brand, and that is the question
 * someone arrives on this page with.
 *
 * Everything inside is contract markup, written the way a consumer writes it (ADR-8), with not one
 * class from this page: the dimension's whole argument is that no component knows it exists, so
 * demonstrating it with hand-rolled markup would demonstrate something else. What is local is the
 * frame (the window, its ratio, its two columns) and the level picker.
 *
 * THE PICKER IS THIS WINDOW'S, NOT THE SITE'S. It writes `data-accent` on the frame its
 * `aria-controls` names and nowhere else, for two reasons that point the same way: a control buried
 * in a figure has no business re-theming the page around it, and a subtree at its own level inside a
 * document at another IS the composition rule this page explains two sections further down. Shown
 * rather than asserted. The header meter stays the site's, and that the two can sit at different
 * levels is the proof, not an oversight.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import "@skryensya/core/components/tile.css";
/* The mock uses pieces the docs shell does not load: each brings its own sheet, which is exactly the
 * import this page would tell a consumer to write. */
import "@skryensya/core/components/tag.css";
import "@skryensya/core/components/checkbox.css";
import "@skryensya/core/components/navbar.css";
import "@skryensya/core/components/list.css";
import "@skryensya/core/components/process-list.css";
import "@skryensya/core/patterns/box.css";
import "@skryensya/core/patterns/image-frame.css";

const enable = `/* An opt-in import, like high contrast or radius. */
@import "@skryensya/core/tokens.scss";
@import "@skryensya/core/dimensions/accent.scss";

/* No toolchain? The bundle already ships it:
   <link rel="stylesheet" href="@skryensya/core/skryensya.css"> */`;

const activate = `<!-- The whole document, one level down. -->
<html data-accent="2">

<!-- Or a subtree: inside a dialled-down zone, a section
     can come back to full reach. -->
<section data-accent="3">…</section>`;

const optIn = `/* A component never reads the raw accent: it names the REASON
   something is brand-coloured, and the dimension does the rest. */
.sk-badge[data-tone="accent"] {
  --sk-badge-bg: var(--color-decorative-bg);
  --sk-badge-fg: var(--color-decorative-fg);
  --sk-badge-dot-color: var(--color-decorative-border);
}

/* Same shape, other reason: this is navigation, not decoration. */
.sk-nav-list__link[aria-current="page"] {
  --sk-nav-list-link-current-bg: var(--color-nav-current-bg);
  --sk-nav-list-link-current-fg: var(--color-nav-current-fg);
}`;
---

<Base
  title="Accent reach"
  description="A dimension over how many things wear the brand: the accent never changes colour, it changes how many places reach for it."
>
  <h1>Accent reach</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An axis over the accent's <strong>spread</strong>, not its colour. At every level the accent is
    the same colour, the same ramp, exactly as saturated. What changes is how many places reach for
    it. A product whose sidebar, table of contents, badges, shortcuts and every link are all
    brand-blue is not more branded than one where only its primary action is; it is just louder, and
    the loudness costs it the ability to point at anything.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="info">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
      <p>
        <strong>It is live, at two different scopes.</strong> The three-bar meter in the header moves
        the whole site: the rail on the left, the index on the right, the badges and the links in this
        prose. The mock below has its own picker and its own level. They are the same dimension applied
        to two subtrees, not one real and one simulated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Three levels</h2>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Level</th>
          <th>The accent appears on</th>
          <th>Goes neutral</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">3</td>
          <td>Everything that uses it today. The baseline, the stylesheet without the attribute.</td>
          <td>Nothing.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">2</td>
          <td>The primary action, state, and prose links.</td>
          <td>Navigation chrome and decoration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">1</td>
          <td>The primary action and state. The brand shows up where you act, nowhere else.</td>
          <td>The above, plus prose links.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="reach-demo">Watch it move</h2>
  <p>
    A whole app, at screen proportions, every piece written the way a consumer writes it. The picker
    below moves <strong>this window only</strong>, not the site: it is the same attribute on a subtree,
    which is exactly what the dimension promises you can do. The header button still moves the rest of
    the page, so you can leave them at different levels and compare.
  </p>

  <figure class="reach-app">
    {/*
      The header stays compact because chrome only needs to advance between states. Here each level
      is described in a selectable Tile: comparing and jumping straight to one is worth more than
      saving width.

      THEY RUN 3 TO 1, like the table above: 3 is the baseline, the sheet with no attribute, and each
      following tile switches off one more group. Read in that order the descriptions subtract from
      one another, which is the axis; reversed they read as three unrelated options. 3 is also the
      default level, so the selected one comes first.

      `aria-controls` names the boundary: the script writes `data-accent` on that region and nowhere
      else.
    */}
    <div class="reach-app__control">
      <p class="reach-app__control-label" aria-hidden="true">Accent reach in the preview</p>
      <div
        class="sk-tile-grid reach-app__accent-options"
        data-sk-tile-radio-group
        data-name="accent-reach"
        data-default-value="3"
        data-orientation="horizontal"
        data-scope="tile"
        aria-label="Accent reach in the preview"
        aria-controls="reach-window"
        data-docs-accent-choice
      >
        <label
          class="sk-tile sk-tile--interactive sk-interactive"
          data-padding="md"
          data-scope="tile"
          data-part="item"
        >
          <input value="3" type="radio" data-part="input">
          <span class="sk-tile__content" data-part="content">
            <span class="sk-tile__content" data-part="content">
              <span class="sk-tile__title">Level 3</span>
              <span class="sk-tile__description">
                Brand colour on the primary button, the links, the current menu
                item and the tags. This is the baseline.
              </span>
            </span>
          </span>
          <span
            class="sk-tile__selection-indicator"
            aria-hidden="true"
            data-part="indicator"
          ></span>
        </label>
        <label
          class="sk-tile sk-tile--interactive sk-interactive"
          data-padding="md"
          data-scope="tile"
          data-part="item"
        >
          <input value="2" type="radio" data-part="input">
          <span class="sk-tile__content" data-part="content">
            <span class="sk-tile__content" data-part="content">
              <span class="sk-tile__title">Level 2</span>
              <span class="sk-tile__description">
                Brand colour on the primary button and the links. The menu item
                and the tags go neutral.
              </span>
            </span>
          </span>
          <span
            class="sk-tile__selection-indicator"
            aria-hidden="true"
            data-part="indicator"
          ></span>
        </label>
        <label
          class="sk-tile sk-tile--interactive sk-interactive"
          data-padding="md"
          data-scope="tile"
          data-part="item"
        >
          <input value="1" type="radio" data-part="input">
          <span class="sk-tile__content" data-part="content">
            <span class="sk-tile__content" data-part="content">
              <span class="sk-tile__title">Level 1</span>
              <span class="sk-tile__description">
                Brand colour on the primary button and on state, like a ticked
                checkbox. The links go neutral too.
              </span>
            </span>
          </span>
          <span
            class="sk-tile__selection-indicator"
            aria-hidden="true"
            data-part="indicator"
          ></span>
        </label>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/*
      The mock. Everything inside is contract markup, with not one class from this page.

      `scripts/accent-showcase.ts` moves it into a srcdoc iframe on init: the mock becomes a DOCUMENT
      of its own, so the documentation's prose CSS stops reaching it (measured: a `p { margin }` rule
      was landing on its paragraphs) and nothing the docs add tomorrow can either. With no JS this
      renders as-is, slightly leakier and perfectly readable: the markup is written once and MOVED,
      never duplicated.
    */}
    <div
      class="reach-app__frame"
      id="reach-window"
      data-accent="3"
      data-docs-accent-frame
      data-frame-title="Atlas, the example app"
    >
      <header class="sk-navbar reach-app__navbar">
        <div class="sk-navbar__brand">Atlas</div>

        {/*
          The nav-list in the bar too, horizontal. It is a SECOND instance of the chrome token
          (`--color-nav-current-*`), and that is why it is here: level 2 quiets chrome and decoration,
          and with a single current item in the whole mock that dimming barely registered. That the
          same pattern serves top and side is not a shortcut of this page, it is ADR-17: the nav-list
          is a guest, and orientation is a variant, not a second structure.
        */}
        <nav class="sk-nav-list" data-orientation="horizontal" aria-label="Atlas views">
          <div class="sk-nav-list__group">
            <ul class="sk-nav-list__list">
              <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo" aria-current="page">
                  <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Dashboard</span>
                </a>
              </li>
              <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo">
                  <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Reports</span>
                </a>
              </li>
              <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo">
                  <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Calendar</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </nav>

        {/*
          Search only. There used to be a loose badge reading "3" and an avatar here, and both had to
          go for different reasons: the number hung off nothing, so it meant nothing (a counter is
          "3 of what"; the contract ships `sk-badge-holder` precisely to hang one off a control, and
          with no bell in the stable icon vocabulary there is no control to hang it off), and the
          bar's flex row stretched it to 26×32, an oval rather than a pill. The avatar was the same
          account already sitting in the rail's footer: an app says who you are ONCE, and this rail
          says it there, the same as this documentation's own.
        */}
        <div class="sk-navbar__actions">
          {/* A resting key IS decoration and dials with the labels: `kbd.css` now reads the decorative
              tokens at rest and the action colour only under `[data-pressed]`, where "this key is
              active" is information and never dials. It is here because this is where a real ⌘K
              lives. */}
          <button class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-variant="ghost" data-size="sm">
            Search
            <kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘K</kbd>
          </button>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="reach-app__body">
        {/* No `sk-sidebar__header`: the brand is already in the navbar, and saying it twice in a
            window this size reads as two apps glued together. The nav-list's group labels are what
            give the rail its structure, the same as this documentation's own. */}
        <aside class="sk-sidebar reach-app__sidebar">
          <div class="sk-sidebar__content sk-scrollbar">
            <nav class="sk-nav-list" data-orientation="vertical" aria-label="Atlas sections">
              <div class="sk-nav-list__group">
                <div class="sk-nav-list__group-label" id="reach-g-project">Project</div>
                <ul class="sk-nav-list__list" aria-labelledby="reach-g-project">
                  <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                    <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo" aria-current="page">
                      <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Overview</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                  <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                    <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo">
                      <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Activity</span>
                      <span class="sk-nav-list__trailing">
                        <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="accent">12</span>
                      </span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                  <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                    <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo">
                      <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Files</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div class="sk-nav-list__group">
                <div class="sk-nav-list__group-label" id="reach-g-team">Team</div>
                <ul class="sk-nav-list__list" aria-labelledby="reach-g-team">
                  <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                    <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo">
                      <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Members</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                  <li class="sk-nav-list__item">
                    <a class="sk-nav-list__link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo">
                      <span class="sk-nav-list__label">Permissions</span>
                    </a>
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </nav>
          </div>
          {/* An account block, not a loose disc next to a name: the avatar at its base size (`md`)
              with the name and the role stacked beside it, which is the shape this has in any app and
              the one that gives the disc something to align to. At `sm` it came out smaller than the
              line of text it sat next to. */}
          <div class="sk-sidebar__footer">
            <div class="sk-inline" data-gap="sm" data-align="center" data-justify="start" data-wrap="true">
              <span class="sk-avatar" data-size="md" aria-label="Ada Lovelace" role="img">
                <span class="sk-avatar__fallback" aria-hidden="true">AL</span>
              </span>
              <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="none">
                <p class="sk-text" data-tone="primary" data-size="sm" data-weight="label">Ada Lovelace</p>
                <p class="sk-text" data-tone="tertiary" data-size="caption" data-weight="body">Editor</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <main class="reach-app__main sk-scrollbar">
          <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="lg">
            <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="xs">
              <div class="sk-inline" data-gap="xs" data-align="center" data-justify="start" data-wrap="true">
                <h2 class="sk-heading" data-size="h3" data-flush>Overview</h2>
                <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="accent">In progress</span>
              </div>
              {/* Several links, not one. Level 2 differs from level 1 ONLY through `--color-text-link`,
                  and with a single loose link in the whole mock that step was a three-word colour
                  change: measured, exactly one node changed between level 2 and level 1. With the
                  links spread out, level 2 reads as what it is, the brand cut back to the action and
                  to whatever you can press in the text. */}
              <p class="sk-text" data-tone="secondary" data-size="sm" data-weight="body">
                Q3 quarter, closing 30 September. Check the <a class="sk-link sk-interactive"
                href="#alcance-demo" data-tone="primary">publishing guide</a> and the <a
                class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo" data-tone="primary">launch
                plan</a> before you send.
              </p>
              <div class="sk-inline" data-gap="xs" data-align="center" data-justify="start" data-wrap="true">
                <span class="sk-tag" data-tone="accent"><span class="sk-tag__label">design</span></span>
                <span class="sk-tag" data-tone="accent"><span class="sk-tag__label">q3</span></span>
                <span class="sk-tag" data-tone="neutral"><span class="sk-tag__label">internal</span></span>
              </div>
            </div>

            {/*
              THE LIST GOES IN A CARD, and that is not decoration: the panel is the frame's `canvas`
              and Avatar paints itself `surface-sunken` while publishing no colour hook, so on canvas
              it sat one rung away and the discs read as smudges. On `surface` they get back the
              contrast the component assumes. It also stops the content being one flat pile: a header
              on the background and a panel holding the records, which is how an app groups this.
            */}
            <div class="sk-box" data-padding="none" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle">
              <ul class="sk-list" data-density="compact">
                <li class="sk-list__item">
                  <span class="sk-list__leading">
                    <span class="sk-avatar" data-size="md" aria-label="Ada Lovelace" role="img">
                      <span class="sk-avatar__fallback" aria-hidden="true">AL</span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__content">
                    <span class="sk-list__title">Ada published the closing report</span>
                    <span class="sk-list__description">Q3 report · 2 hours ago</span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__trailing">
                    <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="success">On track</span>
                  </span>
                </li>
                <li class="sk-list__item">
                  <span class="sk-list__leading">
                    <span class="sk-avatar" data-size="md" aria-label="Rita Levi" role="img">
                      <span class="sk-avatar__fallback" aria-hidden="true">RL</span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__content">
                    <span class="sk-list__title">Rita commented on the draft</span>
                    <span class="sk-list__description">Launch plan · yesterday</span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__trailing">
                    <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="accent">3</span>
                  </span>
                </li>
                <li class="sk-list__item">
                  <span class="sk-list__leading">
                    <span class="sk-avatar" data-size="md" aria-label="Grace Hopper" role="img">
                      <span class="sk-avatar__fallback" aria-hidden="true">GH</span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__content">
                    <span class="sk-list__title">Legal review pending</span>
                    <span class="sk-list__description">Master agreement · due Friday</span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__trailing">
                    <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="danger">Overdue</span>
                  </span>
                </li>
                <li class="sk-list__item">
                  <span class="sk-list__leading">
                    <span class="sk-avatar" data-size="md" aria-label="Leo Ferrer" role="img">
                      <span class="sk-avatar__fallback" aria-hidden="true">LF</span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__content">
                    <span class="sk-list__title">Leo uploaded three files</span>
                    <span class="sk-list__description">Brand assets · 3 days ago</span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-list__trailing">
                    <span class="sk-badge" data-tone="neutral">New</span>
                  </span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            {/*
              The two new decoration consumers, each where it would really appear: the onboarding
              steps (a numbered marker is trim on a static sequence, the same kind of thing as a
              Badge) and the wash over the cover photo. Both read reach tokens now, so both dial at
              level 2 along with the labels and the chrome.
            */}
            <div class="sk-grid" data-gap="md" data-columns="2">
              <div class="sk-box" data-padding="md" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle">
                <div class="sk-stack" data-gap="sm">
                  <p class="sk-text" data-tone="tertiary" data-size="caption" data-weight="label">
                    Getting started
                  </p>
                  <ol class="sk-process-list">
                    <li class="sk-process-list__item">
                      <div class="sk-process-list__content">
                        <span class="sk-process-list__title">Connect the repository</span>
                      </div>
                    </li>
                    <li class="sk-process-list__item">
                      <div class="sk-process-list__content">
                        <span class="sk-process-list__title">Invite your team</span>
                      </div>
                    </li>
                    <li class="sk-process-list__item">
                      <div class="sk-process-list__content">
                        <span class="sk-process-list__title">Publish the first report</span>
                      </div>
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="sk-box" data-padding="none" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle">
                <div
                  class="sk-image-frame"
                  data-aspect="16/9"
                  data-fit="cover"
                  data-position="center"
                  data-radius="top"
                  data-border="none"
                >
                  <img class="sk-image-frame__media" src="/demos/media-gradient.svg" alt="" />
                  <div class="sk-media-caption" data-edge="bottom">
                    <div class="sk-media-gradient" data-strength="lg" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                    <h2 class="sk-heading" data-size="h4" data-flush>Q3 closing report</h2>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            {/* The feed's footer: the link that in a real app opens the full listing. A third consumer
                of `--color-text-link`, and the one that makes level 2 visible below the panel. */}
            <div class="sk-inline" data-gap="sm" data-align="center" data-justify="between" data-wrap="true">
              <p class="sk-text" data-tone="tertiary" data-size="caption" data-weight="body">4 of 27 events</p>
              <a class="sk-link sk-interactive" href="#alcance-demo" data-tone="primary">See all activity</a>
            </div>

            {/* The actions and their option on one row, which is where a "notify on publish" lives:
                floating above as a loose paragraph, it did not read as part of publishing. */}
            <div class="sk-inline" data-gap="sm" data-align="center" data-justify="start" data-wrap="true">
              <button class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-variant="primary" data-size="md">
                Publish
              </button>
              <button class="sk-button sk-interactive" data-sk-button data-variant="ghost" data-size="md">
                Discard
              </button>
              <label class="sk-checkbox">
                <input class="sk-checkbox__input" checked type="checkbox" />
                <span class="sk-checkbox__control sk-interactive" aria-hidden="true">
                  <span class="sk-checkbox__indicator" data-state="checked">
                    <span data-sk-icon="check" data-sk-icon-size="sm"></span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="sk-checkbox__indicator" data-state="indeterminate">
                    <span data-sk-icon="remove" data-sk-icon-size="sm"></span>
                  </span>
                </span>
                <span class="sk-checkbox__label">Notify the team</span>
              </label>
            </div>
          </div>
        </main>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figcaption>
      <strong>These dial at level 2:</strong> the current item in the navbar and the sidebar, the
      Activity counter, the "In progress" badge on the title, the brand tags, the
      <kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘K</kbd> key, the <em>ghost</em> buttons (Search, Discard), the numbered
      markers in Getting started, and the tint of the wash over the cover.
      <strong>These dial at level 1:</strong> the three links in the prose, which go last and only
      because they keep their underline.
      <strong>These never dial:</strong> Publish, the checked box and the "On track" / "Overdue"
      badges, which are the action, state and the feedback roles.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Why the cut falls where it does</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>"Uses the accent" is not one thing.</strong> A checked checkbox is accent because the
    colour is <em>information</em>; a badge is accent because it is decoration; a sidebar link is
    accent because of navigation chrome. Turning the brand down should quiet the decoration and the
    chrome and leave the information alone, and no single token can express that. The levels sort the
    uses by their reason:
  </p>

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Reason</th>
          <th>Where</th>
          <th>Goes quiet at</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Information</strong></td>
          <td>
            Checked checkbox, selected day, active tab, current step, and a key while it is held
            down: "this one is active right now" is a fact, not trim.
          </td>
          <td>Never. Quieting it would be deleting information, not decoration.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>The action</strong></td>
          <td>The primary button, and only that one.</td>
          <td>Never. The one thing a screen most needs to point at.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>The secondary action</strong></td>
          <td>A <em>ghost</em> button's label.</td>
          <td>
            Level 2. A screen carries many of them, and in brand colour they upstaged the very CTA
            the rule above protects. It falls to primary text, never secondary: quieting the accent
            may not cost a control its affordance.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Chrome</strong></td>
          <td>Current item in the sidebar, the navbar and the page index.</td>
          <td>Level 2. Navigation you have already found: the accent here is habit, not help.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Decoration</strong></td>
          <td>
            Badge, Tag, a resting key, a Process list's numbered marker and the Media gradient's
            tint.
          </td>
          <td>Level 2. Coloured trim; the first thing that can go quiet.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Prose links</strong></td>
          <td><code>.sk-link</code> and the bare anchors in the text.</td>
          <td>Level 1, and only because the underline survives the colour.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-callout" data-tone="warning">
    <div class="sk-callout__content">
      <p>
        <strong>The link goes last for a concrete reason.</strong> WCAG 1.4.1 forbids colour from
        being the only signal that something is a link, so level 1 is only legal where the underline
        stays. If your CSS strips the underline from prose links, it has to bring another affordance
        before dialling down to 1.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>What never dials</h2>
  <p>
    The mock already shows it: Publish, the checked box and the feedback badges stay coloured at
    every level, because they are the action, state and the feedback roles, and none of them has a
    reach token. A held-down key is in that group for the same reason. Two more invariants a
    screenshot cannot show matter as much:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The focus ring.</strong> It reads the accent directly and has no reach token, so no
      level can take the colour off it. Turning the brand down is an aesthetic choice and "where is
      my keyboard focus" is not one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The feedback roles.</strong> Danger, success, warning and info appear at no level: a
      warning does not get quieter because the brand did.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The mechanism: a handful of tier-2 tokens</h2>
  <p>
    The dimension has no per-component rules. <code>semantic/_color.scss</code> declares a handful of
    tokens that resolve to the accent by default, the components read those instead of the raw accent
    tokens, and <code>dimensions/accent.scss</code> re-points them per level. That is why the
    dimension owns exactly one layer of the token graph and knows nothing about any component: adding
    a component to the axis is a one-line change in that component, never a change to the dimension.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Token</th>
          <th>Level 3</th>
          <th>Level 2</th>
          <th>Level 1</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-nav-current-bg</td>
          <td class="mono">bg-accent-subtle</td>
          <td class="mono">bg-surface-sunken</td>
          <td class="mono">bg-surface-sunken</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-nav-current-fg</td>
          <td class="mono">text-accent</td>
          <td class="mono">text-primary</td>
          <td class="mono">text-primary</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-decorative-bg</td>
          <td class="mono">bg-accent-subtle</td>
          <td class="mono">bg-surface-sunken</td>
          <td class="mono">bg-surface-sunken</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-decorative-fg</td>
          <td class="mono">text-accent</td>
          <td class="mono">text-secondary</td>
          <td class="mono">text-secondary</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-decorative-border</td>
          <td class="mono">border-accent</td>
          <td class="mono">border-default</td>
          <td class="mono">border-default</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-decorative-wash</td>
          <td class="mono">action-primary</td>
          <td class="mono">text-primary</td>
          <td class="mono">text-primary</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-action-quiet-fg</td>
          <td class="mono">text-accent</td>
          <td class="mono">text-primary</td>
          <td class="mono">text-primary</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">--color-text-link</td>
          <td class="mono">text-accent</td>
          <td class="mono">text-accent</td>
          <td class="mono">text-primary</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    A component opts in by naming the token that matches its reason. That is all it takes for the
    dimension to reach it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={optIn} label="CSS" />

  <h2>Turning it on</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={enable} label="CSS" />
  <CodeBlock code={activate} label="HTML" />
  <p>
    Level 3 restates the baseline values rather than declaring nothing, because a token cannot reset
    itself to its own inherited value: writing it out is what lets a subtree return to full reach
    inside a dialled-down ancestor.
  </p>

  <h2>It composes with the other dimensions</h2>
  <p>
    It is orthogonal to which brand is loaded, to colour mode, high contrast, density and radius. The
    reach tokens reference semantic tokens, and high contrast re-declares those, not these,
    which is why high contrast and level 1 combine without fighting. This dimension does not choose a
    brand either, that is tier 1, the ramp a product declares in
    <a href="/en/dimensions">Four dimensions</a>. It only decides how far the brand you already
    loaded spreads.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { initAccentShowcase } from "../../scripts/accent-showcase";
    initAccentShowcase();
  </script>
</Base>

<style>
  /* A query container: the window needs its AVAILABLE width, not the viewport's, because what
     constrains it is the document column (which changes with the rail and the index, not with the
     screen). `cqw` is the only way to write "16/10 of whatever there is". */
  .reach-app {
    container-type: inline-size;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-stack-sm);
    margin: var(--space-stack-md) 0 var(--space-section);
  }

  /* Cards name what every reach level does before the reader picks it. */
  .reach-app__control {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-stack-xs);
  }

  /* The visible caption names the three choices; the radiogroup carries the same name for assistive
     technology, while the caption itself stays out of the reading order to avoid repetition. */
  .reach-app__control-label {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-tertiary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-label);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /*
   * THE WINDOW, at 16/10: the proportion of an ordinary monitor, not whatever height the content
   * happened to make. It matters for what the page claims, because "how much brand is on screen" is a
   * ratio of colour to surface, and a box cropped to its content always overstates the count. With the
   * proportion held, the emptiness around the content counts towards the neutral side, exactly as it
   * does in a real app.
   *
   * `overflow: clip` so the navbar and the sidebar end against a real edge: without it, a loose shell
   * in the middle of prose reads as dropped parts rather than as a screen.
   *
   * THE HEIGHT IS THE PROPORTION, WITH A FLOOR AND A CEILING, and content that does not fit SCROLLS
   * inside. That is what a real screen does: it does not grow when you add a row, it scrolls. The
   * previous version grew with its content, and with Getting started and the cover in there it was up
   * to 846px, a "screen" taller than it was wide that looked like no monitor at all. The ceiling in
   * `rem` also keeps the window from eating the whole page in a very wide column.
   */
  .reach-app__frame {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    block-size: calc(100cqw / 1.6);
    min-block-size: 20rem;
    max-block-size: 34rem;
    overflow: clip;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    border-radius: var(--radius-surface);
    background: var(--color-bg-canvas);
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* As an iframe the box no longer holds the content, so it no longer holds the grid either: that
     moves inside, onto `.reach-app__screen`. What stays here is what only the parent document can
     know, the height, because `cqw` resolves against `.reach-app` and that container does not exist
     inside the frame. An `iframe` also arrives as `display: inline`; that has to be said too. */
  iframe.reach-app__frame {
    display: block;
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  /* The mock's grid, now inside the frame. The same rule the box had, moved down one level.
     `minmax(0, …)` and not `min-content`: the body row has to be allowed to be SHORTER than its
     content, which is the condition for the panel inside to scroll rather than stretch the window. */
  .reach-app__screen {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    block-size: 100%;
  }

  /* Nothing here. The Navbar already ships its background and its bottom border, and declares no
     `position`, so the page has nothing to re-declare: as the ceiling of this box it behaves exactly
     as it does at the top of an app. What used to be here (a `position: static` that overrode nothing
     and a border the component already paints) was the page rewriting the component over itself. */

  /*
   * `auto` FOR THE RAIL, NEVER A NUMBER. The column said `13rem` and the Sidebar measured its own,
   * `--sk-sidebar-expanded-inline-size`, then 15rem: the rail overflowed its own column by
   * exactly 32px and sat ON TOP of the content, with the title and the buttons running underneath it.
   * Same mistake as the background and the borders, the page restating a number the component already
   * owns, so the fix is the same: stop restating it. (That hook is 13rem today, the very number the
   * page had copied: the two matching is an accident, not a repair.) With `auto` the track is
   * measured by the rail,
   * and if its width changes tomorrow (or it collapses to a rail) the grid follows with no edit here.
   */
  .reach-app__body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  /*
   * There is nothing to re-declare on the Sidebar either, and that is the point.
   *
   * What used to be here (`position: static`, `block-size: auto`, a `border-inline-end`) overrode the
   * component for no reason: the Sidebar declares no `position`, its `block-size: 100%` is exactly
   * what you want inside a stretching grid cell, and it paints its own border through
   * `--sk-sidebar-border-*`. Worse, `block-size: auto` broke its footer's pin to the bottom, which is
   * half of what this shell does. The page had reimplemented the component on top of the component,
   * which is precisely what ADR-8 says not to do.
   */

  /*
   * NO BACKGROUND OF ITS OWN, and that was the bug that made the rail vanish: this panel painted
   * `--color-bg-surface`, which is exactly the background the Sidebar and the Navbar already paint
   * themselves. Three regions in one colour with a `subtle` border between them: the shell read as a
   * single flat surface and the sidebar looked like it was not there.
   *
   * The right layering already came from the components: navbar and sidebar are CHROME and live on
   * `surface`; the content is the screen's well and stays on the frame's `canvas`. The page picks
   * neither colour, it just stops covering the one the component picked.
   *
   * IT SCROLLS, like the panel of a real app, and now it can: while the window sized itself by
   * measuring this content, a scroll container here contributed 0 to the intrinsic height and
   * flattened the window to nothing. With the height fixed nothing is measured any more, so scrolling
   * goes back to being what it always should have been, how a screen holds more than fits.
   */
  .reach-app__main {
    min-block-size: 0;
    padding: var(--space-inset-md);
    overflow-y: auto;
  }

  /* Below ~40rem the window stops pretending to be a monitor: on a phone the mock is one column and
     the sidebar moves above. */
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    /* Portrait, the window stops being a monitor and becomes a phone: a fixed height, tall and
       narrow. Still an EXPLICIT height rather than `auto`, because an iframe has no intrinsic height:
       with `auto` it collapsed to the user agent's 150px and the mock was reduced to a strip. The
       content inside still scrolls exactly the same. */
    .reach-app__frame,
    iframe.reach-app__frame {
      block-size: 36rem;
      min-block-size: 0;
      max-block-size: none;
    }

    .reach-app__body {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    /* Stacked, the rail is a full-width band and its border moves to the bottom. Both are said
       through the hooks the Sidebar publishes, `--sk-sidebar-inline-size` and `--sk-sidebar-border-*`,
       rather than by overriding properties: the bottom border is the only thing the page has to add,
       because the component only knows about the inline side. */
    .reach-app__sidebar {
      --sk-sidebar-inline-size: 100%;
      --sk-sidebar-border-width: 0;
      border-block-end: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
    }
  }

  .reach-app figcaption {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-caption);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The key quoted in the caption aligns with the text instead of stretching it. */
  .reach-app figcaption .sk-kbd {
    vertical-align: baseline;
  }
</style>
